  "note": "Enemy introductions (lane enemy-intros, October 30; design-notebook/Strangedeep/Visual Direction/Visual Vocabulary.md, 'Every new enemy type gets an introduction'). Adam: \"if a player encounters a new enemy, that enemy should get a cool introduction. xcom does it! xcom does it regularly even after the first encounter\". The first time the party sees a type, the camera breaks the three-quarter view for a short reveal of it, built from a few framings picked by the enemy's size and the room (never a hand-made scene, except the first slime feeding on a corpse). Later, a lighter reveal recurs now and then when a fight starts. Played by the camera beats director (CombatCameraBeats), so input waits, any press skips, and the view comes back exactly. Presentation only: it reads committed responses (the response's enemyTypes) and never touches a rule, a command, a save or a gameplay number. Every number here is a draft for Adam: how often recurring reveals fire is open. Adam, October 30, on the first reveals: every reveal moves the whole time (\"it should always have some motion to it, whether pan, zoom, orbit, or a combination\"): each shot's orbit (degrees round the subject over the shot) and push (the share of the distance closed, a perspective camera; an isometric shot only orbits) are eased, a few degrees, never a turn. A reveal camera never sits in or behind a wall or shoots through one (\"the camera should never shoot through a wall\"): a blocked framing falls back to the next, the last a near-top-down view. Every shot cuts in and cuts back: an ease from or to the tactical view would pass through views the wall test never approved. In a narrow space (a corridor) the camera stands along its length at low-to-mid height and pushes in slowly, before an isometric or top-down view is tried. Adam, October 30, on the human's reveal: \"You put him in a closet!\" A reveal's camera and its subject each need clear cells on both sides of the camera line (settings.clearCells; a one-cell space is a closet); a first reveal waits for a body of its type standing in the open. Two cells either side (the lead's brief) left four of five reveals unplayed in the recorded run, so the draft is one; the number is Adam's to set. Adam, October 30, on how often: the introduction plays on the first view of an enemy type, and at about half of the later fight starts with that type (settings.recurringPercent 50; a boss or a large body still always). The lead, October 30, on blue's reveal (a barrel's lid big in the foreground): no prop's bounds come within settings.propNearMeters of a reveal camera, and none covers more than settings.propFrameShareMax of the frame, at the start, middle or end of its motion. Adam, October 31, on blue's reveal: \"we can have a low reveal whats the controversy\". The first reveal seen through a doorway is a low shot (framing doorway-low): the camera may stand in the doorway or just past its threshold, a prop in its lens or between it and the enemy is hidden while the reveal plays and restored after, and a figure in the way is framed past; walls, shut doors and half-cover still rule it out. The prop rule is then a check on the drawn frame, not a veto on the camera.",
